    faqs: [
      { q: "How does JTBD change product roadmap prioritization?", a: "JTBD shifts prioritization from feature parity with competitors to identifying the highest-priority unmet jobs in the target customer's life. Instead of building the feature the competitor just launched, JTBD analysis asks: what job are our best customers unable to complete today, and what would fundamentally change their ability to make progress? This often points to workflow integrations, reporting capabilities, or collaboration features that customers describe in interviews but that don't appear on standard feature request lists." },
      { q: "How do you identify the JTBD for your product?", a: "JTBD are discovered through qualitative research—specifically structured interviews that ask customers to narrate the story of how they first decided they needed a solution, what they tried before purchasing your product, and what specific moment or event triggered their evaluation. The hiring and firing narrative reveals the job. Demographic surveys cannot surface jobs; only rich narrative interviews that capture the circumstances, motivations, and decision process reveal the actual job the customer is trying to accomplish." },
    ],
