    faqs: [
      { q: "How do you drive CRM adoption among sales teams who resist data entry?", a: "Mandatory CRM adoption requires linking CRM data to outcomes salespeople care about: commission calculations, pipeline reviews with management, territory allocation decisions, and deal support resources should all be exclusively based on CRM data. When a salesperson's deal is not visible in the CRM, management shouldn't see it—which means the deal gets no resources, no coaching support, and no recognition. This consistent enforcement drives adoption more reliably than training programs or gamification." },
      { q: "At what company size does a purpose-built CRM replace spreadsheet tracking?", a: "Most businesses benefit from CRM implementation by the time they have 3-5 salespeople with more than 20 active accounts each. Below that threshold, spreadsheets or basic email organization may be sufficient. The inflection point is when deals are being lost because follow-up falls through the cracks, forecasting is unreliable because pipeline visibility is absent, or customer context is lost when a salesperson leaves. These symptoms typically emerge at $2-5M revenue with a small but growing sales team." },
    ],
